NEW_ITEMS = ['*STATUS: OPEN 2026-10-21 -- the walker\'s answer on roadmap 293; not started.*\n\n**296. A level\'s package carries no drawing of its buildings, so neither a reviewer nor a player can scope one out.** The walker, 2026-10-21, asked where a reviewer should see roadmap 293\'s exploded axonometric -- a walker-review page, a level\'s export, or both: "both. It could even get into the game where the players can \'scope out\' the level and see the buildings the level assembles". Read for it (Level Factory\'s export, 2026-10-21, against cold run 9375\'s package): a building reaches a package only through the presentation compose, which calls Deli Counter\'s `portable_building.build_package` and publishes the whole composed package, and the export\'s copy of the composed root, by exclusion, into `lot/<id>/` (`level_factory/packages/exporting/export.py:2160-2175`); a package names a building by Deli Counter\'s file stem and a drawing carries the spec\'s name -- one library building differs, cbp_town_finale_midbalanced_schemafixed, whose `build/<id>.gameplay.json` names it `cbp_town_finale_midbalanced`; which buildings a level stands is recorded only in `site.tscn`\'s instances; a cold run draws nothing for a library building (`build/floorplans/` is the commit gate\'s ignored side effect of `validate.py --all`), and a facade is never drawn (`validate.py:116-120`); no Level Factory test names an `.svg`, and `test_export_standing_lot.py` pins the folders under `lot/`, so a drawing belongs inside `lot/<id>/`. **What would close it:** each building\'s package carrying its drawings, rendered from its spec when it is composed rather than copied from a build folder that may be stale or empty -- the exploded axonometric and its storey plans, a facade\'s or the reason it has none -- kept as files the engine does not import, since an SVG imported as a texture spends every level\'s disk and import time on a screen no game has built: price it before it ships; an index at the package\'s root naming each building the level stands (its node, its id, its spec, its drawings, where it stands); a walker-review page built from a cold run\'s package, so the reviewer sees what ships; and the in-game screen left to the game, the factory\'s part the drawings and the index.\n', '*STATUS: OPEN 2026-10-21 -- found in passing reading the export for roadmap 296; read, not chased.*\n\n**297. Two Level Factory rules ask for a name the files do not carry.** Found reading the export for roadmap 296, read again, not chased. `packages/pipeline/building_library.py`\'s facade rule (its docstring, 129-134) says exactly one complete shell has no validation manifest, cbp_town_finale_midbalanced_schemafixed, and fails open for it -- but it has one, `deli_counter/build/cbp_town_finale_midbalanced.validation.json`, under its spec\'s name, where the library asks for `<id>.validation.json` by the file stem (145), so a facade or demo flag in it would never be read. And `packages/exporting/export.py:1947-1948` adds "validation" to `skip`, which matches file names (`_copy_tree`, its own comment saying so), so it could not leave the `validation/` directory out -- while `ExportProfile.include_validation`, which gates it, defaults true and nothing in Level Factory sets it false (read again). One reads a name nothing writes; the other is a knob with no effect behind a rule that cannot fire. **What would close it:** the library finding a shell\'s validation manifest by the name Deli Counter writes it under (`build/<id>.gameplay.json`\'s `level`, the mapping roadmap 296 needs too), its docstring\'s claim corrected; the export\'s validation rule made a directory rule with a profile that uses it, or removed with the reason the folder ships; a test failing on the old code for each.\n']


def main():
    raw = RM.read_bytes()
    if b"\r\n" in raw:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the roadmap is LF and CRLF was found; nothing written")
    text = raw.decode("utf-8")
    if "\n**296. " in text:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: already applied; nothing written")
    for n, heading, old, new in STATUSES:
        if text.count(heading) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's heading is not there once; nothing written" % n)
        h = text.find(heading)
        s = text.rindex("\n\n", 0, h) + 2
        if text[s:h] != old:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's status block is not the one this was written against; nothing written" % n)
        text = text[:s] + new + text[h:]
        if text.count(new + heading) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's status no longer adjoins its heading; nothing written" % n)
    heads = [int(m.group(1)) for m in re.finditer(r"(?m)^\*\*(\d+)\. ", text)]
    if max(heads) != 295 or heads[-1] != 295:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the last item is not 295; nothing written")
    if not text.endswith("\n") or text.endswith("\n\n"):
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the roadmap does not end on one newline; nothing written")
    for item in NEW_ITEMS:
        text = text + "\n" + item
    for n in (296, 297):
        if len(re.findall(r"(?m)^\*STATUS: [^\n]*\*\n\n\*\*%d\. " % n, text)) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: item %d's status does not adjoin its heading; nothing written" % n)
    RM.write_bytes(text.encode("utf-8"))
    print("roadmap 284 and 293 NARROWED, 296 and 297 filed; now roadmap_status.py --write / --check")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
